      <h2>About Fukuchani Ruins</h2>
      <p>Fukuchani ruins on Zanzibar&#x27;s northwest coast reveal the archaeological remains of a 16th-century Portuguese settlement and earlier Shirazi trading post dating back to the 12th century. Coral stone foundations, mosque remnants, and pottery fragments tell the story of Zanzibar&#x27;s layered maritime history and Swahili coast trade networks. The site is accessible via a scenic beach walk from Kendwa or Nungwi, passing traditional fishing villages. A quiet, off-the-beaten-path historical excursion.</p>

